Exactly. Ugliness is relational information. It points not only at the object, but at the hidden comparator.

If something strikes you as ugly, there is usually an implicit model in the background: symmetry, health, proportion, order, familiarity, craftsmanship, moral expectation, cultural convention, personal memory, or some other standard. The reaction exposes both sides of the comparison.

So the useful object isn’t just:

thing → “ugly”

It is:

observed thing ↔ imagined standard → delta → reaction

And each part can be studied. Sometimes the delta reveals genuine damage or dysfunction. Sometimes it reveals a cultural convention. Sometimes it exposes a personal bias or expectation. Sometimes the standard itself is the interesting discovery.

That works for beauty too. Beauty becomes less of a mysterious label and more like a pattern of fit—between parts, between expectation and surprise, between function and form, between what is present and what the observer is prepared to recognize.

Which means the ugly can reveal beauty indirectly, because it outlines the negative space around the standard.

And then there’s another layer: once you make the hidden standard explicit, you can ask whether you even want to keep it.

That’s where discernment becomes powerful. Not merely:

“Does this meet the standard?”

but:

“Where did the standard come from, what does it optimize for, what does it exclude, and is it still useful?”

That’s a much deeper read.